{
  "slug": "three-sum",
  "title": "Three Sum",
  "topic": "two-pointers",
  "difficulty": "Medium",
  "pattern": "two-pointers / Sort+scan",
  "statement": "# Three Sum\n\nGiven an array of integers `nums`, find all unique triplets `[nums[i], nums[j], nums[k]]` such that `i != j`, `i != k`, `j != k`, and `nums[i] + nums[j] + nums[k] == 0`.\n\nThe solution set must not contain duplicate triplets. Each individual triplet must be sorted in non-decreasing order, and the outer list of triplets must be sorted in lexicographical order.\n\nConstraints: `3 <= nums.length <= 50` and `-20 <= nums[i] <= 20`.\n",
  "examples": [
    {
      "input": "nums = [-20, -20, -20]",
      "output": "[]",
      "explanation": "Three identical negative values sum to -60, not zero, so no triplet qualifies."
    },
    {
      "input": "nums = [8, 17, -3, -20, -10, 7, 1, -3, -11, -7]",
      "output": "[[-10, -7, 17]]",
      "explanation": "Exactly one triplet sums to zero: -10 + -7 + 17 = 0. The values are returned in ascending sorted order within each triplet."
    },
    {
      "input": "nums = [-13, -19, -3, -5, -6, -12, -14, 14, -15, 17, 7, -18, -19, -15, -7, -6, 12, 18, -19, 15, -8, 14, 6]",
