      "explanation": "Multiple distinct triplets each sum to zero; the full list is sorted lexicographically with each triplet internally sorted."
    }
  ],
  "constraints": [
    "Function signature: `function threeSum(nums: number[])`.",
    "Inputs are JSON-serializable values satisfying the bounds stated above."
  ],
  "hints": [
    "Sort the array first so you can use the two-pointer technique on each suffix.",
    "Fix one anchor value, then move two pointers inward to find pairs summing to its negation.",
    "Skip duplicates at the anchor and within the pair search to avoid repeated triplets."
  ],
  "editorial": {
    "approach": "Sort the array, then for each anchor index i use two pointers (left = i+1, right = n-1) to find pairs summing to -nums[i]. Skip duplicate anchors and duplicate pair values to avoid repeated triplets. The brute force checks all triples with a Set for dedup.",
    "why_optimal": "Brute force is O(n^3) examining all C(n,3) triples. The two-pointer sweep is O(n^2): for each anchor the pointers converge in O(n). On 22 cases the optimal ran in 0.85 ms versus 16.7 ms\u2014about 19x\u2014reflecting the quadratic-to-cubic gap on these sizes.",
    "pitfalls": "Skip duplicate anchors (nums[i] == nums[i-1]) to avoid duplicate triplets. After finding a valid pair, skip duplicate left and right values before advancing both pointers."
  },
